A||||6||Patrick Horne||Another Northstar project...||||5||
Z||000000||Patrick Horne||11-22-2001||06:53 PM||||Yep, I'm new to this forum and guess what, there's now one more person trying to put a N* in a Fiero...<P>Okay, just so everybody knows, I've got an '86 SE 3spd auto and a wrecked (but yet running engine) '95 Caddy Eldorado, so that means that I have /all/ of the parts that were on the Caddy...<P>Anyways, pulled the N*, pulled the instrument control panel, the engine wiring harness, the ECM, the brake booster/mstr cyl., and everything else that was salvagable.  I haven't pulled the dash harness yet (the one that connects the ICP to the ECM unit).<P>Original plans were to just port the N* to the Fiero using all the stock parts from the Caddy (listed above) that I could.  Shouldn't be too hard.<P>Two questions, now that I've been looking through the forum:<BR>Has anybody /successfully/ put a 5spd manual transmission into a previously auto Fiero?  Has anybody mated a manual tranny to a N*?  If yes to either one, what parts did it take?<P>Question 2:  How much NOS can a N* handle before it overloads the engine?<P>ALSO - Here's a fun one for you guys to think about.  Thinking about putting a laptop or palmtop computer in-dash to control the output of the engine.  Current thinking is just to incorporate a Palm Pilot into the center consol with several listings of power outputs or performace options, all at the touch of the palm.  This could be done with any engine really, but like I said it's just an idea right now.  I have a couple of friends that know a couple of things on how to do this, so I'm at least at a starting point....<P>Anyways, if anybody's got any info for me about the manual tranny and the N*, lemme know.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Will||11-22-2001||08:32 PM||||The Getrag will bolt to a N* with the exception of on bolt. I made a clamp block to pick up both bolt holes. Pictures here: <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012465.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/012465.html</A> <P>You will turn a Getrag into a cloud of oily shrapnel before you kill a Norhtstar with NOS--provided it's tuned correctly and not detonating.<P>Mounting the N* in the stock Fiero engine location requires removing most/all of the front cradle rail and fabricating a new one. The oil level sensor and the oil filter boss both interfere with the stock cradle rail, and the side of the oil pan itself has about 1/8" of clearance.<P>If you just want more power, sell the Northstar and do a V8 Archie kit. The N* is enough extra pain that you should only do it if you are dead set on having a N*.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Jay||11-22-2001||10:54 PM||||Will I saved all those photos for future reference!<P>------------------<BR>Jay<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig2_copy_1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||11-22-2001||11:10 PM||||Hi there, welcome to the forum!<P>Wow, what an engine for our cars, eh? ;P)<P>First off, two things you want to do (5-speed, and laptop reprograming) basically *REQUIRE* an aftermarket engine computer.<P>...<P>There is one for $1300 that *Includes a complete finished wiring harness* !  <A HREF="http://www.chrfab.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.chrfab.com</A>  (they'll also build your motor up to around 450bhp at 8,000rpm if you ask nicely  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Will? I've had no cradle-fit troubles (but I am using an 88 car.  NOTE: 88 cradles will go into 84-87 cars, with only a few cheap spring adaptor parts from HeldMotorsports(.com) Also, I've only done a test fit so far, not a final. (I'm using solid mounts, so clearances aren't very important, but non-contact is  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You can have an 88 flywheel modified to work with the N* (8, not 6, crank bolts) and use a stock-type heavy duty clutch, or go all out (Quartermaster dual-disc racing clutch is what I am using.)<P>That actually about covers it, the rest is up to you man!  I'd get that engine computer though, the ONLY WAY to use the stock ECU is to use the stock caddy transaxle, with it's own Control Module (they need to talk to each other, or your car will run like crap.)<BR>It'll cost more than $1300 to adapt the stock ECU, I think. (by the time you are done with wiring, etc.)<P>And the Holley Commander 950 can be tuned six ways to China.  Infact, you HAVE to tune it when you first get it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000004||artherd||11-22-2001||11:10 PM||||mmmdoublepast...<p>[This message has been edited by artherd (edited 11-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||11-23-2001||12:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>Will? I've had no cradle-fit troubles (but I am using an 88 car.  NOTE: 88 cradles will go into 84-87 cars, with only a few cheap spring adaptor parts from HeldMotorsports(.com) Also, I've only done a test fit so far, not a final. (I'm using solid mounts, so clearances aren't very important, but non-contact is   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm using an '84-'87 cradle. I have an '88 cradle out back. I'll go measure it tomorrow and see what the difference is.<BR>Cheap? Hmmm... We have different ideas about cheap. An '88 cradle with modern Malibu hub carriers (115 bolt circle) might work better in an '85-'87 chassis than an '88 chassis...||||reg||1
Z||000006||BigTurbo87GT||11-23-2001||11:08 AM||||I'm doing a 87 GT Getrag 5 speed Northstar.<BR>I haven't completed mine yet, but I'm about halfway (with help from Ben and Will, Thanks guys!). My only problem has been the flywheel (which I'm really close to now). I'm going with the CHRFab computer/harness so have at least one thing that will be direct fit.<BR>I didn't have the frame issue's it sounds like Will had either, btw. The front rail does come really close to the left side of of the engine and I had to trim the oil level sender down some though. I also cut off what was the v6 engine mount point. It looks like the easiest thing will be to use a remote oil filter to solve the oil filter interference issue.||||reg||1
